div","args":[{"k":"op","op":"sqrt","args":[{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"id","name":"R.1"},{"k":"id","name":"R.2"}]},{"k":"id","name":"C.1"}]},{"k":"id","name":"C.2"}]}]},{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"id","name":"C.2"},{"k":"op","op":"plus","args":[{"k":"id","name":"R.1"},{"k":"id","name":"R.2"}]}]}]},"global":false,"stored":null,"unit":null,"shown":false}},{"id":"22","left":474,"top":903,"alignX":474,"alignY":919,"disabled":false,"content":{"k":"eval","expr":{"k":"id","name":"Q"},"stored":null,"unit":null}},{"id":"23","left":54,"top":945,"alignX":54,"alignY":961,"disabled":false,"content":{"k":"define","target":{"k":"var","name":"f.3dB"},"value":{"k":"call","fn":{"k":"id","name":"root"},"args":[{"k":"op","op":"plus","args":[{"k":"call","fn":{"k":"id","name":"ampToDb"},"args":[{"k":"call","fn":{"k":"id","name":"H"},"args":[{"k":"id","name":"f"}]}]},{"k":"real","v":3}]},{"k":"id","name":"f"},{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"real","v":100},{"k":"id","name":"Hz"}]},{"k":"op","op":"mult","args":[{"k":"real","v":10},{"k":"id","name":"kHz"}]}]},"global":false,"stored":null,"unit":null,"shown":false}},{"id":"24","left":434,"top":945,"alignX":434,"alignY":961,"disabled":false,"content":{"k":"eval","expr":{"k":"id","name":"f.3dB"},"stored":null,"unit":{"k":"id","name":"Hz"}}}],"texts":[{"id":"1","left":54,"top":72,"width":440,"height":40.5,"text":"An active low-pass filter. A Sallen–Key stage: two resistors, two capacitors and an op-amp that passes low frequencies and rolls off high ones at 40 dB a decade. We describe the circuit once, as a netlist, and let MathMath solve it at every frequency."},{"id":"2","left":54,"top":120.5,"width":440,"height":13.5,"text":"The parts. Standard values from the E12 series."},{"id":"7","left":54,"top":198,"width":440,"height":27,"text":"The circuit. A row for each part: its name, its two nodes and its value. Node 0 is ground; the op-amp O1 lists its + input, − input and output, here wired as a follower."},{"id":"10","left":54,"top":341,"width":440,"height":40.5,"text":"At DC the capacitors are open, so the output, node 4, follows the 1 V input. Give a frequency, and nodeVoltages solves the circuit as phasors there. The gain is the output over the input:"},{"id":"13","left":54,"top":453.5,"width":440,"height":27,"text":"Bode plot: the gain in dB, left, and the phase in degrees, right, at fifty frequencies a decade from 10 Hz to 100 kHz. Hover over a plot to read its points."},{"id":"18","left":54,"top":864,"width":440,"height":27,"text":"The corner. Theory gives the natural frequency and Q of this stage; root finds where the gain is really 3 dB down."},{"id":"25","left":54,"top":983,"width":440,"height":27,"text":"With Q near 0.71 the response is nearly maximally flat, a Butterworth filter, and its −3 dB point sits close to f₀. Try C₁ = 47 nF: Q rises and a peak appears before the roll-off."}],"opaque":[],"origin":0,"tol":0.001,"exactCompare":true,"zeroOverZero":false,"otherRegions":{},"componentOutputs":[]}}